From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:55:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibWeb+LibWebCommon+LibWebView+WebContent: Run the embedder's
 user scripts

A browser's extensions run content scripts in web pages: at document
start, end, or idle, in the page's own world or in an isolated world of
their own, which shares the page's DOM but none of its JavaScript
objects. This adds what WebKit's WKUserScript and WKContentWorld give an
embedder.

The embedder sets a WebView::UserScriptHandler. While it has one, every
document asks the UI process for its scripts as it becomes ready for
scripts, at the same three places the content blocker runs its
scriptlets: navigated documents, child navigables' first about:blank
documents, and popups' first documents. The UI process answers from its
own records of the document's URL, origin, and navigable, never from
what the renderer says. Each Web::HTML::UserScript names its world, its
injection time, and whether it takes a bridge to the UI process, like
the one the pages of an embedder's own scheme get. Only isolated worlds
get one, and the UI process only takes messages from the worlds it gave
a bridge.

An HTML::IsolatedWorld is a realm whose global wraps the document's
Window, in a wrapper world of its own and with a WindowProxy of its own,
as Tests/LibWeb/TestWindowProxyWorld.cpp builds one. Its errors and
unhandled rejections only go to the console, since the page's error and
unhandledrejection handlers would get its objects. Its wrappers are
detached when its document is destroyed.

ScriptBridgeEndpoint now names a world, and the navigable its document
is in, so the embedder can tell its frames apart.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
